.worktrees/
.DS_Store

# Internal planning documents (not tracked)
V2_PLANNING.md
test-logs/

# Per-run pre-mutation snapshots from scripts/upgrade-project.sh
# (code-upgrade-project-5 atomic finalize). Retained for forensics
# under keep-3 retention; not project source.
.claude/upgrade-snapshots/

# BL-177 leak output: must not be TRACKED here. These files arrive when
# _run_idempotent_backfill's vendored-skills block runs projectless and writes
# into the INVOCATION cwd (its `cd ""` is a silent rc-0 no-op under bash 3.2,
# and the block is gated only on the SOURCE dir existing). The canonical
# copies live in templates/generated/skills/ and ship from there, so tracking
# these would duplicate them byte-for-byte.
#
# NOT inert, despite the origin: Claude Code auto-discovers project-scoped
# skills at this path, so while they sit on disk they ARE this repo's project
# skill set. Ignoring keeps them working locally; do not "clean up" by
# deleting without deciding that on purpose (see `## BL-177:`).
#
# Ignored so a `git add -A` cannot commit them again — it did once, in
# `bb69806`, reaching main via PR #275. This disarms that trap; it does NOT
# fix BL-177, and it makes the leak invisible to `git status`.
.claude/skills/

# Linked worktrees for agent/workflow runs. Untracked and NOT ignored until
# 2026-07-27, which left the same `git add -A` trap armed with a far larger
# blast radius: each is a nested checkout whose `.git` is a FILE, so `git add
# -A` records it as a mode-160000 gitlink rather than its contents — dozens
# of them at any moment. The sibling `.worktrees/` rule at the
# top of this file does NOT cover this path (a pattern with no internal slash
# matches only a directory literally named `.worktrees`).
.claude/worktrees/

# BL-199 / R-199-9: tests/test-init-write-perm-preflight.sh::T2 aims a real
# init.sh run at a path INSIDE this checkout to prove the target guard refuses
# before mkdir. On a healthy tree nothing is ever created here — the guard runs
# first — and the test both removes it and arms an EXIT/INT/TERM trap. This
# rule is the third line of defence: if a regression ever does scaffold a
# ~600-file project here and the interpreter dies before cleanup, it must not
# be committable by a `git add -A`.
.bl199-t2-guard-probe/

# BL-236-LEDGER-IGNORE (framework repo) — the MCP tool-usage ledger, same rule
# the scaffold now ships to generated projects via
# templates/generated/gitignore-base.tmpl. Per-session mutable runtime state:
# written by scripts/session-test-gate-check.sh at SessionStart and mutated by
# scripts/track-tool-usage.sh after every MCP tool call. Tracking it dirties the
# tree every session AND makes the MCP gate's ledger shared state that a clone
# inherits — see `## BL-236:` for the measured pre-satisfaction path.
#
# This repo needed the rule too, and did not have it: the branch that fixes the
# tracked-ledger defect for generated projects committed its own ledger here on
# the first `git add -A`. The scaffold's rule does not cover the framework repo.
.claude/tool-usage.json
